



Tantrasāra — Chapter 2
अनुपायप्रकाशनम् — Anupāyaprakāśanam

The Pathless Path: or, how to attain Śiva without doing a single bloody thing.

Right.

Last chapter, we established that your true nature is Śiva.

Infinite consciousness. Completely free. Absolutely sovereign.

Lovely stuff.

Now you’re probably thinking:

“Brilliant. So how do I get there?”

And that’s where you’ve already cocked it up.

Because the first thing we’re discussing is anupāya.

An — without.

Upāya — means, method, approach.

Literally, the method of no method.

Now, imagine someone who’s been absolutely struck by śaktipāta.

The descent of divine power.

And I don’t mean they’ve had a nice meditation, felt a bit peaceful, and started calling themselves enlightened.

I mean Śakti has hit them so profoundly that their entire understanding of reality is ready to turn inside out.

Their guru says something.

Once.

That’s it.

They hear it, comprehend what it actually means, and immediately recognise the truth.

No six-month retreat.

No breathing exercises.

No spending twelve years sitting in a cave trying to get your kuṇḍalinī to cooperate.

Immediate recognition.

And the extraordinary thing is that this isn’t some temporary spiritual experience.

Their samāveśa — immersion into divine consciousness — is nityodita.

Ever arisen. Continuously present.

It doesn’t have to be repeatedly manufactured.

Now, hang on.

You might reasonably object:

“Abhinava, mate, didn’t you just say understanding and reasoning are part of yoga? Surely this person has used some kind of method?”

Excellent question.

Let’s investigate.

First, tell me something.

If the supreme Lord, Parameśvara, is your own Self, svātmā, and is inherently self-luminous, svaprakāśa, what exactly is any method supposed to accomplish?

Seriously.

Let’s go through the possibilities.

Possibility number one: You’re trying to attain your true nature.

Right.

But your true nature is eternal.

Nitya.

How are you going to acquire something that has never been absent?

It’s not a parcel arriving next Tuesday.

It’s what you already are.

So that’s out.

Possibility number two: You’re trying to make Śiva known.

Known to whom?

Consciousness illuminates itself.

It doesn’t require some second consciousness to come along and announce:

“Excuse me, mate, you’re conscious.”

The very awareness through which you’re attempting to discover Śiva is already the self-luminosity of Śiva.

You’re trying to illuminate the illuminator.

Absolute nonsense.

Possibility number three: You’re trying to remove the veil covering Śiva.

Ah, now we’re getting somewhere.

Except we’re not.

Because what exactly could cover the supreme consciousness?

An object?

A thought?

Ignorance?

All of these can appear only through consciousness.

From the standpoint of Śiva’s unrestricted nature, there isn’t some independently existing veil capable of covering him.

So what are you removing?

Possibility number four: You’re trying to enter Śiva.

Enter?

From where?

For there to be an entrance, you’d need someone standing outside and something waiting inside.

But there is no outside.

There is no second reality.

You’re proposing to travel from Śiva to Śiva.

Bit of a pointless journey, isn’t it?

And this brings us to the central absurdity.

Even the method you propose using would have to exist within Śiva.

So how could it stand apart from Śiva and take you to him?

The whole bloody thing is Śiva already.

Every appearance.

Every moment.

Every experience.

One reality.

Cinmātra-tattva.

Consciousness alone.

And this consciousness is not divided by time.

Not chopped up by space.

Not diminished by limiting conditions.

Not imprisoned by forms.

Words cannot properly circumscribe it.

Ordinary instruments of knowledge cannot contain it.

Why?

Because time, space, forms, and the very instruments through which things are known arise through its own freedom.

Svātantrya.

This reality isn’t waiting for permission from something else to exist.

It is utterly independent.

Ānanda-ghana.

A fullness of bliss.

And now comes the crucial recognition.

Tad eva cāham.

That very reality am I.

Not this little personality you’ve spent your whole life constructing.

Not merely your body, your biography, your opinions, or your collection of spiritual accomplishments.

The self-luminous reality in which all of these appear.

And the entire universe?

Viśvaṃ pratibimbitam.

Reflected within that consciousness.

Within me.

Not within my skull.

Within the unrestricted awareness that I actually am.

Now, when this is discerned with unshakeable certainty, there’s no further method necessary.

The immersion in Parameśvara is already present.

So for such a person, there is no compulsory dependence on mantra.

No compulsory dependence on pūjā.

No compulsory dependence on meditation.

No compulsory dependence on elaborate religious observances.

Not because these things are rubbish.

We’ll discuss their value in subsequent chapters.

But because a means is unnecessary when the end is already fully realised.

And Abhinavagupta finishes with an absolutely beautiful image.

He asks, in effect:

“Can a little clay pot be responsible for illuminating the thousand-rayed sun?”

Of course it bloody can’t.

The sun is already shining.

You could assemble every lamp, every candle, every instrument of illumination in the entire universe.

None of them would contribute a single ray to the sun’s own radiance.

And likewise, no collection of spiritual techniques can produce the self-luminous Śiva.

For one who genuinely sees this, divine immersion is immediate.

Kṣaṇāt.

In an instant.

And that’s anupāya.

The pathless path.

Not that you’ve finally found the perfect technique.

But that there was never any distance to travel.

